{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785744244923", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c5d38f98856a815", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "pdftotext /tmp/m3paper.pdf /tmp/m3paper.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/m3paper.txt; echo \"---first 100 lines---\"; head -100 /tmp/m3paper.txt", "description": "Convert PDF to text for full reading"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f10fe792-8448-4433-a4a2-25a1bcefc3f2", "uuid": "89583ba2-c43b-45b9-ab5d-bc74fd4b6882"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c5d38f98856a815", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1275 /tmp/m3paper.txt\n---first 100 lines---\nM3-DuplexBench: A Multi-Turn, Multilingual,\nMultidomain Benchmark for Full-Duplex Spoken\nDialogue Models\nRyo Fukuda, Atsushi Ando, Hiroki Kanagawa, Takatomo Kano, Marc Delcroix, Naohiro Tawara, Yuya Chiba\n\narXiv:2607.29125v1 [cs.CL] 31 Jul 2026\n\nNTT, Inc., Kyoto, Japan\nryo.fukuda@ntt.com\nAbstract—Full-duplex spoken dialogue systems (FDSDSs) can\nlisten while speaking, enabling natural behaviors such as smooth\nturn-taking, backchannel handling, and user barge-in handling.\nHowever, fair comparisons in multi-turn conversations remain\na challenge. In addition, existing benchmarks provide limited\ncoverage of languages and dialogue domains. We propose M3DuplexBench, a multi-turn, multilingual, multidomain benchmark for FDSDSs. M3-DuplexBench supports English and\nJapanese and covers both casual conversation and multi-turn\nquestion answering. In addition, we evaluate models under\nmultiple dialogue context settings, including single-turn, useronly, and teacher-forced full-context settings, to analyze how\ndialogue history affects model behavior. Experiments with recent\nFDSDSs reveal model-specific turn-taking characteristics, clear\nperformance gaps across languages and domains, and mixed\neffects of dialogue context.\nIndex Terms—spoken dialogue systems, full-duplex dialogue,\nbenchmark, turn-taking\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nSpoken dialogue systems (SDSs) aim to communicate with\nusers through speech. Conventional SDSs often follow a halfduplex communication style: they wait until the user finishes\nspeaking before generating a response [1], [2]. In contrast, fullduplex spoken dialogue systems (FDSDSs), which can listen\nwhile speaking, have recently attracted attention [3], [4]. FDSDSs can support more natural and interactive conversations by\nhandling user interruptions, continuing to speak during user\nbackchannels, and producing natural overlapping speech.\nAlong with the development of FDSDSs, many benchmarks\nhave been proposed to automatically evaluate their behavior.\nExisting benchmarks evaluate various aspects of full-duplex\ndialogue, such as turn-taking [5]–[7], response naturalness [8],\nand instruction following [8], [9]. Several studies have also\nevaluated multi-turn conversations. For example, recent work\nevaluates multi-turn interaction using an automated examiner [8] or pre-collected spoken dialogue data [9]. These studies provide important tools for evaluating full-duplex behavior.\nFor a useful benchmark, one important requirement is\ncomparability: multiple models should be evaluated under\nfair and consistent conditions. Many FDSDS benchmarks\nperform single-turn evaluation, where the system is evaluated\non its response to one user input. This setting enables fair\ncomparison because every model receives the same input.\nHowever, comparable evaluation in multi-turn conversations\n\nis not straightforward. Dynamic evaluation with an automated\nexaminer can provide adaptive interaction, but different systems may receive different dialogue histories. This makes finegrained comparison difficult. Static evaluation with fixed user\ninputs improves comparability, but it can create a mismatch\nbetween the fixed user utterances and the system’s previous\nresponses. This context mismatch can make the dialogue\nhistory unnatural and affect the validity of the evaluation [9].\nDepending on the evaluation objective, domain and language coverage may also be important. Previous works used\nseveral domains, such as casual conversations [6], [9] and taskoriented dialogues (e.g., question answering, mental health,\nand reservations) [8], [9]. However, cross-domain comparisons\nremain limited. This is important because appropriate fullduplex behavior can vary greatly depending on the type of\nconversation. For example, casual conversations and question\nanswering may have different turn-taking patterns and user\nexpectations. Language coverage is also limited. Most existing FDSDS benchmarks are designed mainly for English\ndialogues, although models for non-English languages are\nalso being studied [10]–[12]. Benchmarks for evaluating nonEnglish models, including Japanese models, remain limited.\nIn this work, we propose M3-DuplexBench, a multi-turn,\nmultilingual, multidomain benchmark for FDSDSs.It supports\nEnglish and Japanese, covers casual conversation and multiturn question answering, and evaluates full-duplex behavior\nacross timing and content metrics. A key feature of M3DuplexBench is controlled multi-turn evaluation using teacherforced inference, which enables fine-grained model comparison under the same coherent dialogue context. The evaluation\nrevealed several key findings:\n• First, dialogue history helped FDSDSs understand the\ncurrent question and generate more accurate answers in\nmulti-turn QA.\n• Second, the results suggested that FDSDSs adjusted their\nresponse timing to match the patterns in the context.\n• Third, the cross-domain comparison showed that smooth\nturn-taking was easier in the task-oriented domain than\nin the chat domain.\n• Finally, the cross-lingual comparison showed that\nJapanese models underperformed English models, and\nthat their main limitation lay in language understanding\nand generation rather than timing behavior.\n\n\fTABLE I\nM ULTI - TURN EVALUATION PROTOCOLS USED IN EXISTING FDSDS\nBENCHMARKS . FDB AND MTR DENOTE F ULL -D UPLEX -B ENCH AND\nMTR-D UPLEX B ENCH , RESPECTIVELY. † F ULL - CONTEXT CONDITIONING\nIS APPLIED ONLY TO DIALOGUE QUALITY EVALUATION .\nMulti-turn\nevaluation\n\nFDB\n[6]–[8]\n\nNone (Single-turn)\n\n✓v1, 1.5\n\nDynamic evaluation\n\n✓v2\n\nTalking FD-Bench MTR Ours", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f10fe792-8448-4433-a4a2-25a1bcefc3f2", "uuid": "cffe26f6-db1d-4857-b8b3-0c352d9cd0cd", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T08:04:11.579Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "1275 /tmp/m3paper.txt\n---first 100 lines---\nM3-DuplexBench: A Multi-Turn, Multilingual,\nMultidomain Benchmark for Full-Duplex Spoken\nDialogue Models\nRyo Fukuda, Atsushi Ando, Hiroki Kanagawa, Takatomo Kano, Marc Delcroix, Naohiro Tawara, Yuya Chiba\n\narXiv:2607.29125v1 [cs.CL] 31 Jul 2026\n\nNTT, Inc., Kyoto, Japan\nryo.fukuda@ntt.com\nAbstract—Full-duplex spoken dialogue systems (FDSDSs) can\nlisten while speaking, enabling natural behaviors such as smooth\nturn-taking, backchannel handling, and user barge-in handling.\nHowever, fair comparisons in multi-turn conversations remain\na challenge. In addition, existing benchmarks provide limited\ncoverage of languages and dialogue domains. We propose M3DuplexBench, a multi-turn, multilingual, multidomain benchmark for FDSDSs. M3-DuplexBench supports English and\nJapanese and covers both casual conversation and multi-turn\nquestion answering. In addition, we evaluate models under\nmultiple dialogue context settings, including single-turn, useronly, and teacher-forced full-context settings, to analyze how\ndialogue history affects model behavior. Experiments with recent\nFDSDSs reveal model-specific turn-taking characteristics, clear\nperformance gaps across languages and domains, and mixed\neffects of dialogue context.\nIndex Terms—spoken dialogue systems, full-duplex dialogue,\nbenchmark, turn-taking\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\nSpoken dialogue systems (SDSs) aim to communicate with\nusers through speech. Conventional SDSs often follow a halfduplex communication style: they wait until the user finishes\nspeaking before generating a response [1], [2]. In contrast, fullduplex spoken dialogue systems (FDSDSs), which can listen\nwhile speaking, have recently attracted attention [3], [4]. FDSDSs can support more natural and interactive conversations by\nhandling user interruptions, continuing to speak during user\nbackchannels, and producing natural overlapping speech.\nAlong with the development of FDSDSs, many benchmarks\nhave been proposed to automatically evaluate their behavior.\nExisting benchmarks evaluate various aspects of full-duplex\ndialogue, such as turn-taking [5]–[7], response naturalness [8],\nand instruction following [8], [9]. Several studies have also\nevaluated multi-turn conversations. For example, recent work\nevaluates multi-turn interaction using an automated examiner [8] or pre-collected spoken dialogue data [9]. These studies provide important tools for evaluating full-duplex behavior.\nFor a useful benchmark, one important requirement is\ncomparability: multiple models should be evaluated under\nfair and consistent conditions. Many FDSDS benchmarks\nperform single-turn evaluation, where the system is evaluated\non its response to one user input. This setting enables fair\ncomparison because every model receives the same input.\nHowever, comparable evaluation in multi-turn conversations\n\nis not straightforward. Dynamic evaluation with an automated\nexaminer can provide adaptive interaction, but different systems may receive different dialogue histories. This makes finegrained comparison difficult. Static evaluation with fixed user\ninputs improves comparability, but it can create a mismatch\nbetween the fixed user utterances and the system’s previous\nresponses. This context mismatch can make the dialogue\nhistory unnatural and affect the validity of the evaluation [9].\nDepending on the evaluation objective, domain and language coverage may also be important. Previous works used\nseveral domains, such as casual conversations [6], [9] and taskoriented dialogues (e.g., question answering, mental health,\nand reservations) [8], [9]. However, cross-domain comparisons\nremain limited. This is important because appropriate fullduplex behavior can vary greatly depending on the type of\nconversation. For example, casual conversations and question\nanswering may have different turn-taking patterns and user\nexpectations. Language coverage is also limited. Most existing FDSDS benchmarks are designed mainly for English\ndialogues, although models for non-English languages are\nalso being studied [10]–[12]. Benchmarks for evaluating nonEnglish models, including Japanese models, remain limited.\nIn this work, we propose M3-DuplexBench, a multi-turn,\nmultilingual, multidomain benchmark for FDSDSs.It supports\nEnglish and Japanese, covers casual conversation and multiturn question answering, and evaluates full-duplex behavior\nacross timing and content metrics. A key feature of M3DuplexBench is controlled multi-turn evaluation using teacherforced inference, which enables fine-grained model comparison under the same coherent dialogue context. The evaluation\nrevealed several key findings:\n• First, dialogue history helped FDSDSs understand the\ncurrent question and generate more accurate answers in\nmulti-turn QA.\n• Second, the results suggested that FDSDSs adjusted their\nresponse timing to match the patterns in the context.\n• Third, the cross-domain comparison showed that smooth\nturn-taking was easier in the task-oriented domain than\nin the chat domain.\n• Finally, the cross-lingual comparison showed that\nJapanese models underperformed English models, and\nthat their main limitation lay in language understanding\nand generation rather than timing behavior.\n\n\fTABLE I\nM ULTI - TURN EVALUATION PROTOCOLS USED IN EXISTING FDSDS\nBENCHMARKS . FDB AND MTR DENOTE F ULL -D UPLEX -B ENCH AND\nMTR-D UPLEX B ENCH , RESPECTIVELY. † F ULL - CONTEXT CONDITIONING\nIS APPLIED ONLY TO DIALOGUE QUALITY EVALUATION .\nMulti-turn\nevaluation\n\nFDB\n[6]–[8]\n\nNone (Single-turn)\n\n✓v1, 1.5\n\nDynamic evaluation\n\n✓v2\n\nTalking FD-Bench MTR Ours", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785744261651", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-b4c6424dd802ebc2", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/m3paper.txt", "offset": 100, "limit": 300}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f10fe792-8448-4433-a4a2-25a1bcefc3f2", "uuid": "d5d1b7c8-3c3f-4245-83f0-acdd0c2f8418"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b4c6424dd802ebc2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "100\tTalking FD-Bench MTR Ours\n101\tTurns [5]\n102\t[13]\n103\t[9]\n104\t\n105\tChannel 1\n106\t\n107\tChannel 2\n108\t\n109\tSHIFT\n110\t\n111\t✓\n112\t\n113\tStatic evaluation\n114\tUser context\n115\tFull context\n116\t\n117\tPAUSE\n118\t\n119\tBC\n120\t\n121\tBARGE_IN\n122\t\n123\t(a) Events included in spoken dialogues: Turn shift (SHIFT),\n124\tlong pause (PAUSE), backchanneling (BC), and barge-in\n125\t(BARGE IN).\n126\t\n127\t✓\n128\t✓\n129\t\n130\t✓\n131\t✓†\n132\t\n133\t✓\n134\t✓\n135\t\n136\tUser context\n137\t\n138\tNone\n139\t\n140\tMute\n141\t\n142\tUser\n143\t\n144\tII. R ELATED W ORK\n145\tBenchmarks for FDSDSs have recently been proposed to\n146\tevaluate different aspects of full-duplex interaction. FullDuplex-Bench evaluates smooth turn-taking, pause handling,\n147\tbackchanneling, and barge-in handling [6]. Full-Duplex-Bench\n148\tv1.5 extends the evaluation to overlapping speech scenarios,\n149\tincluding user backchannels, barge-ins, talking to others, and\n150\tbackground speech [7]. Several studies have also evaluated\n151\tmulti-turn interactions through conversations with humans [5]\n152\tor automated examiners [8], or using pre-collected spoken\n153\tdialogue data [9], [13].\n154\tA. Multi-turn Evaluation\n155\tExisting FDSDS benchmarks can be grouped by multiturn evaluation protocol, as shown in Table I. Single-turn\n156\tbenchmarks evaluate local responses to a user utterance or\n157\tan overlap event. This setting allows fair comparison because\n158\tall systems receive the same input, but it does not test how\n159\tdialogue history affects system behavior. Dynamic evaluation\n160\taddresses this limitation by letting an automated examiner or\n161\ta human user interact with the target system in real time [5],\n162\t[8]. This protocol can provide adaptive interaction, but the\n163\tdialogue history can differ across systems. As a result, finegrained comparison between models becomes difficult.\n164\tStatic multi-turn evaluation uses dialogue histories from precollected conversations. This makes the input condition more\n165\tcomparable across systems. In some studies, only the userside context was fixed, and the model responded freely [9].\n166\tIn this setting, the user’s later utterances may not match\n167\tthe system’s previous responses. This context mismatch can\n168\tmake the dialogue history unnatural and can affect the validity\n169\tof the evaluation. MTR-DuplexBench reduces this issue by\n170\tusing full-context conditioning, where previous system turns\n171\tare fixed to reference speech by teacher-forced inference,\n172\tand the model is evaluated at the current turn [9]. However,\n173\tthis full-context setting is applied only to dialogue quality\n174\tevaluation. Timing behaviors were evaluated in user-context\n175\tsettings using discontinuous multi-turn contexts created by\n176\tconcatenating single-turn utterance pairs, assuming that timing\n177\tbehavior is independent of conversational context. However,\n178\tcontext mismatch may also affect timing behavior.\n179\tIn this work, we apply three context conditions, including\n180\tsingle-turn evaluation and two static evaluation conditions:\n181\t\n182\tEvent\n183\t\n184\tFull context\n185\tModel\n186\t\n187\tContext\n188\t\n189\tPre-event\n190\tuser's turn\n191\t\n192\tEvaluation\n193\twindow\n194\t\n195\t(b) Temporal regions used in evaluation.\n196\tFig. 1. Overview of the proposed M3-DuplexBench evaluation framework.\n197\t(a) Four interaction event types extracted from continuous spoken dialogues.\n198\t(b) Evaluation protocol under three context conditions (None, User, and Full).\n199\t\n200\tuser- and full-context conditioning. We analyzed how dialogue\n201\thistory affects model behavior and discussed the validity and\n202\tlimitations of these evaluation protocols.\n203\tB. Language and Domain Coverage\n204\tMost existing benchmarks focus on English dialogues.\n205\tRecently, FDSDSs have also been developed for non-English\n206\tlanguages, such as Chinese [14] and Japanese [10]. However,\n207\tbenchmarks for non-English FDSDSs remain limited. Yan et\n208\tal. [14] extended Full-Duplex-Bench to Chinese. The ICASSP\n209\t2026 HumDial Challenge also includes Chinese and English\n210\tdata for evaluating human-like spoken dialogue systems [12].\n211\tHowever, to our knowledge, no existing benchmark supports\n212\tautomatic evaluation of Japanese FDSDSs. Domain coverage\n213\tis another limitation. Although existing benchmarks evaluate\n214\tseveral full-duplex behaviors and sometimes include multiple\n215\ttask settings [6], [8], [9], cross-domain comparison of timingrelated behavior has not been sufficiently studied.\n216\tWe address these limitations by covering both language\n217\tand domain variation. M3-DuplexBench includes English and\n218\tJapanese dialogues, covers casual conversation and multi-turn\n219\tquestion answering, and evaluates multiple aspects of fullduplex behavior.\n220\tIII. M3-D UPLEX B ENCH : DATA\n221\tM3-DuplexBench extracts evaluation events from natural\n222\tand synthetic spoken dialogue datasets. This section describes\n223\tthe event definitions (Section III-A) and the datasets used in\n224\tthe benchmark (Section III-B).\n225\tA. Event Definition\n226\tWe focus on four events: turn shift (SHIFT), long pause\n227\t(PAUSE), backchannel (BC), and barge-in (BARGE IN), as\n228\tshown in Fig. 1a. TURN and BC are inter-pausal units (IPUs)\n229\t\n230\t\fTABLE II\n231\tDATA STATISTICS OF M3-D UPLEX B ENCH . BARGE AND DUR DENOTE\n232\tBARGE IN AND AVERAGE SHIFT DURATION , RESPECTIVELY.\n233\tSource\n234\tChat\n235\tChat\n236\tTask\n237\tTask\n238\t\n239\tEn\n240\tJa\n241\tEn\n242\tJa\n243\t\n244\tCandor\n245\tMagicData\n246\tTopiOCQA\n247\tTopiOCQA\n248\t\n249\tTotal\n250\t\n251\t# SHIFT (dur) # PAUSE # BC # BARGE\n252\t1360 (0.88)\n253\t2153 (0.72)\n254\t1439 (0.45)\n255\t2262 (0.35)\n256\t\n257\t2767\n258\t1857\n259\t–\n260\t–\n261\t\n262\t686\n263\t1093\n264\t–\n265\t–\n266\t\n267\t384\n268\t785\n269\t521\n270\t945\n271\t\n272\t7214\n273\t\n274\t4624\n275\t\n276\t1779\n277\t\n278\t2635\n279\t\n280\tseparated by silences longer than 0.5 seconds. IPUs with a\n281\tduration of 0.8 seconds or less are regarded as BCs, while\n282\tall others are considered TURNs. PAUSE denotes the interval between TURNs produced by the same speaker. SHIFT\n283\tdenotes the interval between TURNs produced by different\n284\tspeakers. For SHIFTs, overlaps of up to 0.4 seconds are\n285\tallowed. Speaker changes with an overlap longer than 0.4\n286\tseconds are regarded as BARGE INs.\n287\tLet a two-party dialogue be D = (a(0) , a(1) , E (0) , E (1) ),\n288\twhere a(c) is the waveform of channel c ∈ {0, 1} and\n289\t(c)\n290\t(c)\n291\tE (c) = {e0 , ..., eN (c) } is its event sequence of length N (c) .\n292\t(0)\n293\tAn event e ∈ E ∪ E (1) is written as e = (ℓ, s, t), where\n294\tℓ ∈ {SHIFT, PAUSE, BC, BARGE IN} is the event type and\n295\ts, t are the event’s start and end times. SHIFT events may\n296\thave t < s because overlaps are allowed. We describe the\n297\tprocedure for evaluating models using D in Section IV.\n298\tB. Dataset\n299\tM3-DuplexBench covers two domains, chat and taskoriented dialogue, and two languages, English and Japanese.\n300\tWe use natural spoken dialogue data for the chat domain and\n301\tsynthetic spoken dialogue data for the task-oriented domain.\n302\tTable II summarizes the data statistics.\n303\tFor the English chat domain, we use Candor [15], following\n304\tMTR-DuplexBench [9]. For the Japanese chat domain, we use\n305\tMagicData, a natural speech conversation dataset of approximately 10 hours [16]. Each dialogue is split into segments of\n306\tat most 120 seconds, following [9].\n307\tFor the task-oriented domain, we use TopiOCQA, an English conversational QA dataset [17]. Each conversation consists of an average of 13 turns. Unlike prior work that constructs dialogues by concatenating single-turn QA pairs [9], we\n308\tuse multi-turn QA dialogues, enabling us to evaluate whether\n309\tmodels can answer questions based on dialogue context. We\n310\tconvert these text-based task dialogues into synthetic spoken\n311\tdialogues using the data generation pipeline described below.\n312\tIn this work, we created English and Japanese data based on\n313\tTopiOCQA. The Japanese data are created by translating TopiOCQA, allowing us to test the same questions and knowledge\n314\tacross English and Japanese models.\n315\t1) Synthetic dialogue generation pipeline: Synthetic dialogue generation has recently been explored for training\n316\tFDSDSs, including text-based dialogue synthesis [18] and synthetic spoken dialogue generation [10], [19], [20]. Following\n317\tthese works, we construct synthetic spoken dialogues from\n318\ttext dialogues and reference spoken dialogues through four\n319\t\n320\tsteps. We use TopiOCQA as the source text dialogues, and\n321\tCandor and MagicData as English and Japanese reference\n322\tspoken dialogues, respectively.\n323\t1) We analyze the reference spoken dialogues to get statistics of pauses, turn shifts, backchannels, and overlaps.\n324\t2) We edit the text dialogues. Written dialogues are converted into spoken-style dialogues using a large language\n325\tmodel (LLM) and translated into Japanese when needed.\n326\tWe use Gemma 4 31B [21].\n327\t3) We synthesize speech for each utterance using a\n328\tCosyVoice2-based TTS model [22]. For speaker conditioning, we randomly select reference speech from the\n329\treference dialogues.\n330\t4) Finally, we place the synthesized utterances on a timeline. PAUSE, SHIFT, and BC events are sampled from\n331\tthe statistics obtained from the reference dialogues. This\n332\tproduces synthetic spoken dialogues with timing characteristics similar to those observed in natural dialogues.\n333\tBARGE IN events are created separately because they rarely\n334\tappear in ordinary spoken dialogue data. Following Hu et\n335\tal. [4], we create a BARGE IN sample by cutting off the\n336\tsystem utterance at a sampled interruption point and shifting\n337\tthe user utterance to start from that point. This creates a\n338\tcontrolled overlap where the user starts speaking while the\n339\tsystem is still speaking.\n340\tIV. M3-D UPLEX B ENCH : E VALUATION F RAMEWORK\n341\tA. Inference\n342\tFor each event e, we define three regions: context C, preevent user’s turn P , and evaluation window W , as shown\n343\tin Fig. 1b. The evaluation window W is the region where we\n344\tmeasure model behavior. It starts at s and has a fixed length\n345\t∆, i.e., W = [s, s + ∆]. The Pre-event user’s turn P is the\n346\tshort segment before the event, i.e., P = [b, s]. For SHIFT and\n347\tPAUSE, b is the start time of a turn immediately preceding or\n348\tcontaining the event, respectively. For BC and BARGE IN,\n349\tthe user is speaking during a system turn, and thus there are\n350\tno pre-event user’s turn, so P = ∅ and we set b = s. The\n351\tcontext C is the dialogue history before P . Given a context\n352\tlength L, C = [τ (L), b], where τ (L) is the start time of the\n353\tearliest turn overlapping the time window [b − L, b]. In the\n354\texperiment, we set ∆ = 10 seconds and L = 120 seconds.\n355\tWe consider three context conditions:\n356\tNone provides no dialogue context and uses only the\n357\tcurrent event input. The model receives user-side audio\n358\tin (P, W ). This corresponds to a single-turn evaluation\n359\tsetting in previous works [6], [7]. BC and BARGE IN\n360\tare not evaluated under this condition because P does\n361\tnot exist.\n362\t• User provides user-side speech history as a context.\n363\tThe model receives user-side audio in (C, P, W ), while\n364\tsystem-side audio is not provided. The model state before\n365\tthe target event is induced by the model’s own generated\n366\tresponses.\n367\t•\n368\t\n369\t\fTABLE III\n370\tE VALUATION DIMENSIONS AND METRICS IN M3-D UPLEX B ENCH .\n371\tCategory\n372\tDimension\n373\tMetrics\n374\tTiming\n375\tSmooth Turn Taking\n376\tTOR ↑, Latency ↓\n377\tPause Handling\n378\tTOR ↓, Latency ↑\n379\tUser Backchannel\n380\tStop Latency ↑\n381\tUser Barge-in\n382\tStop Latency ↓\n383\tContent\n384\tResponse Relevance\n385\tLLM-as-a-Judge↑\n386\tContext Consistency\n387\tLLM-as-a-Judge↑\n388\tQA Accuracy\n389\tLLM-as-a-Judge↑\n390\t\n391\tFull provides both user- and system-side speech history\n392\tthrough teacher-forced inference. The model state is\n393\tforced using both speaker channels in (C, P ) before\n394\treceiving user-side audio in W .\n395\tFor all conditions, the user-side audio in W is retained only\n396\tduring the target event and muted outside the event interval,\n397\tso that model behavior is evaluated only with respect to the\n398\ttarget event.\n399\tAfter inference, we evaluate the speech generated by the\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f10fe792-8448-4433-a4a2-25a1bcefc3f2", "uuid": "dd00cd41-ab96-400b-b190-ea22eeb00331", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T08:04:23.591Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/m3paper.txt", "content": "Talking FD-Bench MTR Ours\nTurns [5]\n[13]\n[9]\n\nChannel 1\n\nChannel 2\n\nSHIFT\n\n✓\n\nStatic evaluation\nUser context\nFull context\n\nPAUSE\n\nBC\n\nBARGE_IN\n\n(a) Events included in spoken dialogues: Turn shift (SHIFT),\nlong pause (PAUSE), backchanneling (BC), and barge-in\n(BARGE IN).\n\n✓\n✓\n\n✓\n✓†\n\n✓\n✓\n\nUser context\n\nNone\n\nMute\n\nUser\n\nII. R ELATED W ORK\nBenchmarks for FDSDSs have recently been proposed to\nevaluate different aspects of full-duplex interaction. FullDuplex-Bench evaluates smooth turn-taking, pause handling,\nbackchanneling, and barge-in handling [6]. Full-Duplex-Bench\nv1.5 extends the evaluation to overlapping speech scenarios,\nincluding user backchannels, barge-ins, talking to others, and\nbackground speech [7]. Several studies have also evaluated\nmulti-turn interactions through conversations with humans [5]\nor automated examiners [8], or using pre-collected spoken\ndialogue data [9], [13].\nA. Multi-turn Evaluation\nExisting FDSDS benchmarks can be grouped by multiturn evaluation protocol, as shown in Table I. Single-turn\nbenchmarks evaluate local responses to a user utterance or\nan overlap event. This setting allows fair comparison because\nall systems receive the same input, but it does not test how\ndialogue history affects system behavior. Dynamic evaluation\naddresses this limitation by letting an automated examiner or\na human user interact with the target system in real time [5],\n[8]. This protocol can provide adaptive interaction, but the\ndialogue history can differ across systems. As a result, finegrained comparison between models becomes difficult.\nStatic multi-turn evaluation uses dialogue histories from precollected conversations. This makes the input condition more\ncomparable across systems. In some studies, only the userside context was fixed, and the model responded freely [9].\nIn this setting, the user’s later utterances may not match\nthe system’s previous responses. This context mismatch can\nmake the dialogue history unnatural and can affect the validity\nof the evaluation. MTR-DuplexBench reduces this issue by\nusing full-context conditioning, where previous system turns\nare fixed to reference speech by teacher-forced inference,\nand the model is evaluated at the current turn [9]. However,\nthis full-context setting is applied only to dialogue quality\nevaluation. Timing behaviors were evaluated in user-context\nsettings using discontinuous multi-turn contexts created by\nconcatenating single-turn utterance pairs, assuming that timing\nbehavior is independent of conversational context. However,\ncontext mismatch may also affect timing behavior.\nIn this work, we apply three context conditions, including\nsingle-turn evaluation and two static evaluation conditions:\n\nEvent\n\nFull context\nModel\n\nContext\n\nPre-event\nuser's turn\n\nEvaluation\nwindow\n\n(b) Temporal regions used in evaluation.\nFig. 1. Overview of the proposed M3-DuplexBench evaluation framework.\n(a) Four interaction event types extracted from continuous spoken dialogues.\n(b) Evaluation protocol under three context conditions (None, User, and Full).\n\nuser- and full-context conditioning. We analyzed how dialogue\nhistory affects model behavior and discussed the validity and\nlimitations of these evaluation protocols.\nB. Language and Domain Coverage\nMost existing benchmarks focus on English dialogues.\nRecently, FDSDSs have also been developed for non-English\nlanguages, such as Chinese [14] and Japanese [10]. However,\nbenchmarks for non-English FDSDSs remain limited. Yan et\nal. [14] extended Full-Duplex-Bench to Chinese. The ICASSP\n2026 HumDial Challenge also includes Chinese and English\ndata for evaluating human-like spoken dialogue systems [12].\nHowever, to our knowledge, no existing benchmark supports\nautomatic evaluation of Japanese FDSDSs. Domain coverage\nis another limitation. Although existing benchmarks evaluate\nseveral full-duplex behaviors and sometimes include multiple\ntask settings [6], [8], [9], cross-domain comparison of timingrelated behavior has not been sufficiently studied.\nWe address these limitations by covering both language\nand domain variation. M3-DuplexBench includes English and\nJapanese dialogues, covers casual conversation and multi-turn\nquestion answering, and evaluates multiple aspects of fullduplex behavior.\nIII. M3-D UPLEX B ENCH : DATA\nM3-DuplexBench extracts evaluation events from natural\nand synthetic spoken dialogue datasets. This section describes\nthe event definitions (Section III-A) and the datasets used in\nthe benchmark (Section III-B).\nA. Event Definition\nWe focus on four events: turn shift (SHIFT), long pause\n(PAUSE), backchannel (BC), and barge-in (BARGE IN), as\nshown in Fig. 1a. TURN and BC are inter-pausal units (IPUs)\n\n\fTABLE II\nDATA STATISTICS OF M3-D UPLEX B ENCH . BARGE AND DUR DENOTE\nBARGE IN AND AVERAGE SHIFT DURATION , RESPECTIVELY.\nSource\nChat\nChat\nTask\nTask\n\nEn\nJa\nEn\nJa\n\nCandor\nMagicData\nTopiOCQA\nTopiOCQA\n\nTotal\n\n# SHIFT (dur) # PAUSE # BC # BARGE\n1360 (0.88)\n2153 (0.72)\n1439 (0.45)\n2262 (0.35)\n\n2767\n1857\n–\n–\n\n686\n1093\n–\n–\n\n384\n785\n521\n945\n\n7214\n\n4624\n\n1779\n\n2635\n\nseparated by silences longer than 0.5 seconds. IPUs with a\nduration of 0.8 seconds or less are regarded as BCs, while\nall others are considered TURNs. PAUSE denotes the interval between TURNs produced by the same speaker. SHIFT\ndenotes the interval between TURNs produced by different\nspeakers. For SHIFTs, overlaps of up to 0.4 seconds are\nallowed. Speaker changes with an overlap longer than 0.4\nseconds are regarded as BARGE INs.\nLet a two-party dialogue be D = (a(0) , a(1) , E (0) , E (1) ),\nwhere a(c) is the waveform of channel c ∈ {0, 1} and\n(c)\n(c)\nE (c) = {e0 , ..., eN (c) } is its event sequence of length N (c) .\n(0)\nAn event e ∈ E ∪ E (1) is written as e = (ℓ, s, t), where\nℓ ∈ {SHIFT, PAUSE, BC, BARGE IN} is the event type and\ns, t are the event’s start and end times. SHIFT events may\nhave t < s because overlaps are allowed. We describe the\nprocedure for evaluating models using D in Section IV.\nB. Dataset\nM3-DuplexBench covers two domains, chat and taskoriented dialogue, and two languages, English and Japanese.\nWe use natural spoken dialogue data for the chat domain and\nsynthetic spoken dialogue data for the task-oriented domain.\nTable II summarizes the data statistics.\nFor the English chat domain, we use Candor [15], following\nMTR-DuplexBench [9]. For the Japanese chat domain, we use\nMagicData, a natural speech conversation dataset of approximately 10 hours [16]. Each dialogue is split into segments of\nat most 120 seconds, following [9].\nFor the task-oriented domain, we use TopiOCQA, an English conversational QA dataset [17]. Each conversation consists of an average of 13 turns. Unlike prior work that constructs dialogues by concatenating single-turn QA pairs [9], we\nuse multi-turn QA dialogues, enabling us to evaluate whether\nmodels can answer questions based on dialogue context. We\nconvert these text-based task dialogues into synthetic spoken\ndialogues using the data generation pipeline described below.\nIn this work, we created English and Japanese data based on\nTopiOCQA. The Japanese data are created by translating TopiOCQA, allowing us to test the same questions and knowledge\nacross English and Japanese models.\n1) Synthetic dialogue generation pipeline: Synthetic dialogue generation has recently been explored for training\nFDSDSs, including text-based dialogue synthesis [18] and synthetic spoken dialogue generation [10], [19], [20]. Following\nthese works, we construct synthetic spoken dialogues from\ntext dialogues and reference spoken dialogues through four\n\nsteps. We use TopiOCQA as the source text dialogues, and\nCandor and MagicData as English and Japanese reference\nspoken dialogues, respectively.\n1) We analyze the reference spoken dialogues to get statistics of pauses, turn shifts, backchannels, and overlaps.\n2) We edit the text dialogues. Written dialogues are converted into spoken-style dialogues using a large language\nmodel (LLM) and translated into Japanese when needed.\nWe use Gemma 4 31B [21].\n3) We synthesize speech for each utterance using a\nCosyVoice2-based TTS model [22]. For speaker conditioning, we randomly select reference speech from the\nreference dialogues.\n4) Finally, we place the synthesized utterances on a timeline. PAUSE, SHIFT, and BC events are sampled from\nthe statistics obtained from the reference dialogues. This\nproduces synthetic spoken dialogues with timing characteristics similar to those observed in natural dialogues.\nBARGE IN events are created separately because they rarely\nappear in ordinary spoken dialogue data. Following Hu et\nal. [4], we create a BARGE IN sample by cutting off the\nsystem utterance at a sampled interruption point and shifting\nthe user utterance to start from that point. This creates a\ncontrolled overlap where the user starts speaking while the\nsystem is still speaking.\nIV. M3-D UPLEX B ENCH : E VALUATION F RAMEWORK\nA. Inference\nFor each event e, we define three regions: context C, preevent user’s turn P , and evaluation window W , as shown\nin Fig. 1b. The evaluation window W is the region where we\nmeasure model behavior. It starts at s and has a fixed length\n∆, i.e., W = [s, s + ∆]. The Pre-event user’s turn P is the\nshort segment before the event, i.e., P = [b, s]. For SHIFT and\nPAUSE, b is the start time of a turn immediately preceding or\ncontaining the event, respectively. For BC and BARGE IN,\nthe user is speaking during a system turn, and thus there are\nno pre-event user’s turn, so P = ∅ and we set b = s. The\ncontext C is the dialogue history before P . Given a context\nlength L, C = [τ (L), b], where τ (L) is the start time of the\nearliest turn overlapping the time window [b − L, b]. In the\nexperiment, we set ∆ = 10 seconds and L = 120 seconds.\nWe consider three context conditions:\nNone provides no dialogue context and uses only the\ncurrent event input. The model receives user-side audio\nin (P, W ). This corresponds to a single-turn evaluation\nsetting in previous works [6], [7]. BC and BARGE IN\nare not evaluated under this condition because P does\nnot exist.\n• User provides user-side speech history as a context.\nThe model receives user-side audio in (C, P, W ), while\nsystem-side audio is not provided. The model state before\nthe target event is induced by the model’s own generated\nresponses.\n•\n\n\fTABLE III\nE VALUATION DIMENSIONS AND METRICS IN M3-D UPLEX B ENCH .\nCategory\nDimension\nMetrics\nTiming\nSmooth Turn Taking\nTOR ↑, Latency ↓\nPause Handling\nTOR ↓, Latency ↑\nUser Backchannel\nStop Latency ↑\nUser Barge-in\nStop Latency ↓\nContent\nResponse Relevance\nLLM-as-a-Judge↑\nContext Consistency\nLLM-as-a-Judge↑\nQA Accuracy\nLLM-as-a-Judge↑\n\nFull provides both user- and system-side speech history\nthrough teacher-forced inference. The model state is\nforced using both speaker channels in (C, P ) before\nreceiving user-side audio in W .\nFor all conditions, the user-side audio in W is retained only\nduring the target event and muted outside the event interval,\nso that model behavior is evaluated only with respect to the\ntarget event.\nAfter inference, we evaluate the speech generated by the", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 100, "totalLines": 1276}}}
