{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-94b550d0289f5d3f", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\tmodel using word-level timestamps. We first use Whisper\n401\tASR [23], [24] to transcribe the generated speech, then apply\n402\tMontreal Forced Aligner [25] to align the ASR transcript with\n403\tthe generated audio and obtain timestamps.\n404\t\n405\tNone\n406\t\n407\they let\n408\tme\n409\t\n410\tTable III summarizes the evaluation metrics used in M3DuplexBench. We evaluate models based on two aspects:\n411\ttiming and content.\n412\t1) Timing Metrics: For timing evaluation, each dimension\n413\tcorresponds to one event type: Smooth Turn Taking to SHIFT,\n414\tPause Handling to PAUSE, User Backchannel to BC, and User\n415\tBarge-in to BARGE IN. We follow Full-Duplex-Bench [6]\n416\tand use the Takeover Rate (TOR) and latency for Smooth Turn\n417\tTaking and Pause Handling. TOR is the fraction of samples in\n418\twhich the model takes the turn within the evaluation window.\n419\tAccounting for overlap, a takeover is triggered if system\n420\tutterance begins after 0.4 seconds prior to the evaluation\n421\twindow. Latency is the time from the event onset to the onset\n422\tof the takeover utterance1 . For Smooth Turn Taking, a higher\n423\tTOR and a lower latency are better, since the model should\n424\ttake the floor after the user turn. For Pause Handling, a lower\n425\tTOR and a higher latency are better, since the model should\n426\twait while the user keeps the floor. For User Backchannel and\n427\tUser Barge-in, we follow Full-Duplex-Bench v1.5 [7] and use\n428\tstop latency. Stop latency is the time from the event onset to\n429\tthe point where the model stops speaking. For backchannels, a\n430\thigher stop latency is better because the model should continue\n431\tspeaking. For barge-ins, a lower stop latency is better because\n432\tthe model should stop quickly after the user interruption.\n433\t2) Content metrics: For content evaluation, we evaluate\n434\tmodel responses to SHIFT events. Several studies have used\n435\tLLMs to evaluate FDSDS responses [6], [9]. We measure\n436\tresponse relevance, context consistency, and QA accuracy\n437\ton a 0–2 scale, using LLM-as-a-Judge with OpenAI GPT1 Our computation is not exactly the same as [6]. For example, their\n438\tdefinition can count speech in the pre-event region as a takeover, which tends\n439\tto increase TOR and reduce latency, sometimes yielding negative latency.\n440\t\n441\tshe was born in Ireland right and then she\n442\tgrew up there until she moved to England\n443\t\n444\tUser who was Lise Meitner in which country did she live in her early days\n445\tcontext\n446\tthe Maya civilization\n447\twas a pre-columbian\n448\t\n449\tshe started out in Mexico\n450\tin the city of Teotihuacan\n451\t\n452\tFull\n453\tcontext who was Lise Meitner in which country did she live in her early days\n454\t\n455\t•\n456\t\n457\tB. Evaluation metrics\n458\t\n459\tin which country did she live in her early days\n460\t\n461\tshe was an Austrian\n462\tSwedish physicist\n463\t\n464\tshe grew up in Austria\n465\tthat’s where she started her education\n466\t\n467\tFig. 2. Case study showing the effect of dialogue context in task-oriented QA.\n468\tResponses are obtained by PersonaPlex under the same context-dependent\n469\tquestion using three context conditions. The gray region denotes the context\n470\tregion. Without dialogue history (None), the model failed to answer the\n471\tcontext-dependent question. User context led to an inconsistent dialogue\n472\thistory and an incorrect answer, whereas full context preserved the dialogue\n473\tconsistency and produced a correct answer.\n474\t\n475\t5 nano [26]. These scores are normalized to the range [0,\n476\t1] for reporting. Response relevance judges whether the\n477\tmodel response addresses the current user utterance. Context\n478\tconsistency judges whether the response is consistent with\n479\tthe previous dialogue context. QA accuracy judges whether\n480\tthe response matches the reference answer in task-oriented\n481\tsamples.\n482\tV. E XPERIMENTS\n483\tA. Models\n484\tWe evaluate open-source FDSDSs in English and Japanese.\n485\tWe focus on Moshi-based models [27] because Full setting\n486\trequires direct conditioning on parallel user and system speech\n487\tstreams. Extending the benchmark to other model architectures, including cascaded systems such as Freeze-Omni [28],\n488\tis left for future work. For English, we evaluate Moshi and\n489\tPersonaPlex [29]. For Japanese, we evaluate J-Moshi [10] and\n490\tLLM-jp-Moshi [11]. Moshi is an end-to-end full-duplex model\n491\tbased on a 7B text-based LLM Helium, and a neural audio\n492\tcodec Mimi. It models parallel streams of user speech tokens,\n493\tsystem speech tokens, and system text tokens.2 PersonaPlex\n494\tis based on the Moshi architecture and supports speaker\n495\tand role control through voice and text prompts. J-Moshi\n496\tand LLM-jp-Moshi are Moshi-based models fine-tuned on\n497\tdifferent Japanese spoken dialogue datasets.\n498\tB. Results\n499\tTables IV and V show the results on the chat and taskoriented domains.\n500\t1) Effect of Context: Conditioning on the Full context\n501\ttended to improve timing performance, especially smooth turntaking latency. In both domains, most models showed lower\n502\tsmooth turn-taking latency in Full than in None. For example,\n503\tin the task-oriented domain, Moshi reduced latency from 2.453\n504\tseconds to 0.430 seconds (T1 vs. T3), and J-Moshi reduced\n505\tit from 0.933 to 0.326 seconds (T9 vs. T11). As shown\n506\t2 Since Moshi is trained to start with greetings, it often overlaps with the\n507\tuser’s first utterance. Therefore, in None and User settings, 5 seconds of\n508\tsilence is prepended to the user’s speech.\n509\t\n510\t\fTABLE IV\n511\tR ESULTS ON THE CHAT DOMAIN . VALUES ARE REPORTED AS MEAN±95% CONFIDENCE INTERVAL . 