one who likes math if math formulas are turned into words..)\n808\t(10) この賞はなぜ重要なんでしょうか この賞の人たちはどのような人たちなのでしょうか\n809\t(why is this award important what kind of people are associated with it)\n810\t\n811\t(b) Japanese examples.\n812\tFig. 3. Case studies of model responses in multi-turn QA. In these examples,\n813\tEnglish models generated responses relevant to the current question, whereas\n814\tJapanese models produced less relevant responses, showing the cross-lingual\n815\tgap in context-dependent answer generation.\n816\t\n817\tin Table II, the average SHIFT duration (i.e., smooth turntaking latency) in the evaluation data was 0.88 seconds, 0.72\n818\tseconds, 0.45 seconds, and 0.35 seconds for English chat,\n819\tJapanese chat, English task-oriented dialogue, and Japanese\n820\ttask-oriented dialogue, respectively. We found that the latency\n821\tin Full was often closer to these values than in None. This\n822\tsuggests that the FDSDSs can adjust their response timing\n823\tto match the patterns in the dialogue context. The effect\n824\tof Full context on smooth turn-taking TOR was mixed, and\n825\tit often degraded performance in the chat domain (e.g., C1\n826\tvs. C3). Chat conversations contain many ambiguous turn\n827\t\n828\ttransitions, optional responses, and diverse interaction patterns.\n829\tThis may have caused a gap with the model’s natural generation trajectories. The Full context also improved the content\n830\tmetrics of English models. For example, in the task-oriented\n831\tdomain, PersonaPlex improved QA accuracy from 0.180 in\n832\tNone to 0.306 in Full (T5 vs. T7). This suggests that dialogue\n833\thistory helped models understand the current question\n834\tand generate more accurate answers in multi-turn QA.\n835\tFig. 2 shows an example of comparing the three contextual\n836\tconditions in the task-oriented domain, highlighting that the\n837\tfull context preserved dialogue consistency and improved\n838\tresponse quality. In contrast, Japanese models showed only\n839\tlimited content improvements under Full (e.g., C9 vs. C11),\n840\tindicating that providing context did not necessarily lead to\n841\taccurate answer generation.\n842\tConditioning only on user context (User) was unstable in\n843\tmany cases. Across domains and models, smooth turn-taking\n844\tTOR generally decreased, and latency showed inconsistent\n845\ttrends. Prior work evaluated multi-turn timing under User\n846\tcontext conditioning and reported that timing metrics degraded\n847\tas the number of turns increased [9]. However, our results\n848\tshowed that the Full setting often outperformed None in timing\n849\tmetrics. This suggests that the degradation observed under\n850\tUser context conditioning may not only reflect the inherent\n851\tdifficulty of multi-turn interaction, but may also be caused\n852\tby context mismatch between the fixed user-side history\n853\tand the model’s own generated history. The Full setting\n854\talso has limitations: it requires direct conditioning on parallel\n855\tuser and system speech streams, which limits the range of applicable models, and may introduce a gap between the teacherforced trajectory and the model’s natural inference trajectory.\n856\tNevertheless, given the instability of the User setting, we\n857\tconsider Full to be a more reliable condition for static multi-\n858\t\n859\t\fTABLE V\n860\tR ESULTS ON THE TASK - ORIENTED DOMAIN . M ETRICS , NOTATIONS , AND REPORTING FORMAT ARE IDENTICAL TO THOSE IN TABLE IV.\n861\t\n862\tJapanese task-oriented\n863\t\n864\tEnglish task-oriented\n865\t\n866\tTiming\n867\tID\n868\t\n869\tModel\n870\t\n871\tContext\n872\t\n873\tT1\n874\tT2\n875\tT3\n876\t\n877\tMoshi\n878\t\n879\tNone\n880\tUser\n881\tFull\n882\t\n883\tT4\n884\tT5\n885\tT6\n886\tT7\n887\t\n888\tNone&Full\n889\tPersonaPlex\n890\t\n891\tT8\n892\tT9\n893\tT10\n894\tT11\n895\t\n896\tNone&Full\n897\tJ-Moshi\n898\t\n899\tT12\n900\tT13\n901\tT14\n902\tT15\n903\tT16\n904\t\n905\tNone\n906\tU