pause-handling TOR was\n1101\t0.306, which was higher than Moshi’s 0.152 (C8 vs. C4).\n1102\tThis indicates that PersonaPlex responded quickly, but was\n1103\talso more likely to take the turn during user-held pauses.\n1104\tThe stop latency of barge-in was generally higher than\n1105\tthat of back-channeling, suggesting that the models’ ability\n1106\tto distinguish user backchannels and user barge-ins to some\n1107\textent. However, barge-in stop latency still remained around\n1108\t1.7–2.6 seconds for several models, indicating that real-time\n1109\tinterruption handling remains challenging.\n1110\tAmong the Japanese models, LLM-jp-Moshi showed better\n1111\ttiming performance. In the chat domain, its smooth turn-taking\n1112\tTOR was 0.875, outperforming J-Moshi’s 0.645. In contrast,\n1113\tJ-Moshi achieved better content scores in both domains.\n1114\t3) Cross-domain Difference: Across domains, smooth\n1115\tturn-taking was easier in the task-oriented domain than\n1116\tin the chat domain. Most models achieved higher TOR and\n1117\tlower latency in task-oriented dialogue. For example, Moshi’s\n1118\tsmooth turn-taking TOR increased from 0.629 in the chat\n1119\tdomain to 0.916 in the task-oriented domain (C4 vs. T4). This\n1120\tlikely reflects the structure of task-oriented QA, where user\n1121\tutterances are often explicit questions and the expected timing\n1122\tof system responses is clearer. In contrast, chat contains more\n1123\tambiguous turn transitions, optional responses, and diverse\n1124\tresponse candidates, making it more difficult for models to\n1125\tdecide when to speak.\n1126\t\n1127\t4) Cross-lingual Difference: The cross-lingual gap was\n1128\tclear in the content metrics for the task-oriented domain.\n1129\tEnglish models achieved higher response relevance and QA\n1130\taccuracy than Japanese models. For example, PersonaPlex\n1131\tobtained 0.848 in response relevance and 0.243 in QA accuracy, while J-Moshi obtained 0.557 and 0.136 (T8 vs.\n1132\tT12). Since the English and Japanese task-oriented evaluation\n1133\tsets were generated from the same QA data, these results\n1134\tsuggest that Japanese models still lag behind English models in language understanding and context-dependent answer\n1135\tgeneration. Fig. 3 shows examples of English and Japanese\n1136\tmodel responses to the same question.\n1137\tOn the other hand, Japanese models showed competitive\n1138\ttiming performance. For example, J-Moshi showed smaller\n1139\tlatencies of smooth turn-taking in both domains compared\n1140\tto Moshi (e.g., T12 vs.T4). These results suggest that the\n1141\tmain limitation of current Japanese full-duplex models\n1142\tlies in content understanding and generation, rather than\n1143\tin timing behavior.\n1144\tVI. C ONCLUSION\n1145\tIn this work, we present M3-DuplexBench, a novel benchmark for FDSDSs. The benchmark covers English and\n1146\tJapanese, chat and task-oriented dialogue, and evaluates both\n1147\ttiming and content through event-level samples extracted from\n1148\tcontinuous spoken conversations. By comparing multiple context conditions, M3-DuplexBench enables controlled analysis\n1149\tof how dialogue history affects FDSDSs. Our experiments\n1150\tshow that dialogue context greatly affects both timing and\n1151\tcontent evaluation. In particular, user-only conditioning can\n1152\tintroduce context mismatch, whereas full-context conditioning\n1153\tprovides a more reliable protocol for controlled multi-turn\n1154\tevaluation. Our future work will extend this benchmark to\n1155\tinclude more languages, domains and models.\n1156\t\n1157\t\fVII. G ENERATIVE AI U SE D ISCLOSURE\n1158\tThis manuscript was edited and polished with the assistance\n1159\tof generative AI. All experimental design, implementation,\n1160\tand analysis were conducted by the authors who take full\n1161\tresponsibility for the content.\n1162\tR EFERENCES\n1163\t[1] Y. Chen and H. Yu, “From turn-taking to synchronous dialogue:\n1164\tA survey of full-duplex spoken language models,” arXiv preprint\n1165\tarXiv:arXiv:2509.14515, 2025.\n1166\t[2] G. Skantze, “Turn-taking in conversational systems and human-robot\n1167\tinteraction: A review,” Computer Speech & Language, vol. 67, 2021.\n1168\t[3] T. A. Nguyen, E. Kharitonov, J. Copet, Y. Adi, W.-N. Hsu, A. Elkahky,\n1169\tP. Tomasello, R. Algayres, B. Sagot, A. Mohamed et al., “Generative\n1170\tspoken dialogue language modeling,” Transactions of the Association\n1171\tfor Computational Linguistics, vol. 11, pp. 250–266, 2023.\n1172\t[4] K. Hu, E. Hosseini-Asl, C. Chen, E. Casanova, S. Ghosh, P. Żelasko,\n1173\tZ. Chen, J. Li, J. Balam, and B. Ginsburg, “Efficient and Direct Duplex\n1174\tModeling for Speech-to-Speech Language Model,” in 26th Annual\n1175\tConference of the International Speech Communication Association\n1176\t(INTERSPEECH 2025), 2025, pp. 2715–2719.\n1177\t[5] S. Arora, Z. Lu, C.-C. Chiu, R. Pang, and S. Watanabe, “Talking\n1178\tturns: Benchmarking audio foundation models on turn-taking dynamics,”\n1179\tin 13th International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR\n1180\t2025), 2025.\n1181\t[6] G.-T. Lin, J. Lian, T. Li, Q. Wang, G. Anumanchipalli, A. H. Liu, and H.Y. Lee, “Full-duplex-bench: A benchmark to evaluate full-duplex spoken\n1182\tdialogue models on turn-taking capabilities,” in 2025 IEEE Automatic\n1183\tSpeech Recognition and Understanding Workshop (ASRU), 2025, pp.\n1184\t1–8.\n1185\t[7] G.-T. Lin, S.-Y. S. Kuan, Q. Wang, J. Lian, T. Li, S. Watanabe, and\n1186\tH.-y. Lee, “Full-duplex-bench v1.5: Evaluating overlap handling for\n1187\tfull-duplex speech models,” in 2026 IEEE International Conference on\n1188\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP 2026). IEEE, 2026,\n1189\tpp. 19 447–19 451.\n1190\t[8] G.-T. Lin, S.-Y. S. Kuan, J. Shi, K.-W. Chang, S. Arora, S. Watanabe, and\n1191\tH.-y. Lee, “Full-duplex-bench-v2: A multi-turn evaluation framework for\n1192\tduplex dialogue systems with an